prompt:
You write small CSS patch files that fix measured
accessibility/visual defects in an existing web application.  Answer
with CSS rules only.  The patch will be appended after the existing
stylesheets, so later rules override earlier ones.

Example from a DIFFERENT app (cooking blog).  Measured defect:
"button height 19px, below the 24px minimum".  The correct patch was:

/* fix: raise button hit area */
.blog-btn {
  min-height: 28px;
  padding: 6px 14px;
}
.blog-btn-icon {
  min-width: 28px;
  min-height: 28px;
}


Now YOUR task.  Measured defect (from the automated checker):
at 320px width the page now uses 9 distinct font sizes (typography limit is 8).  The extra size is the 17px media-query h2: at 320px the sizes include both 17 (h2) and 16 (body).  Setting the narrow-screen h2 to 16px keeps the hierarchy (h1=19 > h2=16 > h3=14) and collapses into the body size.

The relevant existing CSS is:
@media (max-width: 720px) {
  .view-head h2 {
    font-size: 17px;
  }
}

Write a patch that adjusts the narrow-screen heading size.
Add ONE media query @media (max-width: 720px) containing a rule for
the selector .view-head h2 with font-size: 16px;  Do not change any
other selector or property.

Write the patch now.  The first line is already written; continue from it:
/* fix: narrow heading size */
@media (max-width: 720px) {

text:
/* fix: narrow heading size */
@media (max-width: 720px) {
.view-head h2 {
    font-size: 16px;
  }
}
